WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is
a Grammy Award-winning musician

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.303
and founding member
of The Roots,

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:06.472
who you can see every night
on "The Tonight Show."

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.140
His fantastic documentary,

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.444
"Summer of Soul
(...or, When the Revolution
Could Not Be Televised)"

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.714
is nominated for an Oscar
for Best Documentary Feature

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:17.516
and is streaming now on Hulu.
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:19.552
-We were constantly
being attacked

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.188
because we weren't
"Black enough."

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:23.456
Sometimes we were called

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.758
"the Black group
with the white sound."

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:27.260
We didn't like that.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.495
We happened to be artists
who are Black

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:31.564
and our voices sound
the way they sound

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.333
and how do you color --
that used to be one

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.402
of our questions --
how do you color a sound?

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:40.273
That was one of the reasons
why performing in Harlem

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:43.175
was so important to us.
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:45.144
-[crying]
Because we wanted our people

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.080
to know what we were about
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.349
-and we were hoping
that they would receive us.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.118
-♪ Come on, let ♪

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:55.387
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Questlove, everybody!

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:58.558
[ Cheering and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:07.366 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:15.374 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:18.811 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:20.980
Welcome back!
-How are you doing?

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.316
-A lot has changed since the
last time you were here.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.118
We were talking about this film
coming out

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.654
and, since then, you won
a BAFTA, just this weekend,

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.255
-Yeah, yeah.
-Spirit Award.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.424
[ Cheering and applause ]
Nominated for a Grammy.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.026
Nominated for an Oscar.
How does it feel?

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:35.762
-Phew!
I'm living the dream.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.896
-Yeah.
-This is living the dream.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.465
Every day it's, you know --

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:40.666
I'm about to say something
cliché like,

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:41.668
"Every day is a winding road."

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.702
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:46.138
No, but it literally is because,
you know,

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:51.277
at least with the music world,
I know, you know --

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.312
Using a Frisbee -- is that
too much of a '70s term?

00:01:53.312 --> 00:01:55.148
-No, you can use that.
It's "Summer of Soul."

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.516
You can use a Frisbee.
-Okay, okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.718
No, I'm just saying that I don't
know how, you know.

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:02.288
At least I know, with the music,
what a goal is,

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:05.024
but in the movie world like
I just had no clue.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.393
I just wanted to make
something really cool

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.762
and, hopefully, they were going
to show it in a few classrooms

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:12.131
and then maybe discover it
20 years from now,

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:13.665
like, "Yo, you know
Questlove did this thing?"

00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:15.535
[ Laughter ]
And, you know, so.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:17.469
-Well, you know, we were just
talking to Kenneth Branagh,

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:18.905
who, obviously, was
Oscar-nominated as well.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.106
He said that you immediately
start hearing from everybody.

00:02:21.106 --> 00:02:22.975
Did you hear
from so many people?
-Oh, man.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.745
The amount of cold calling.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.247
You know, like, especially now,
in the age of like

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:31.351
where like bots
call your iPhone,

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.019
you don't know who to trust
and not to trust.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.755
But one day I was like really,
really hungry

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:39.058
and my Postmates guy was taking
a little bit too long.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.294
-Yeah.
-And so, you know,

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:43.896
it was like an hour
and 10 minutes.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.898
-That's too long, yeah.

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:47.033
-I decided to ring the phone.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.934
Even, you know,
even if it was a bot,

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.704
I was going to answer it
and, you know,

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.039
the person didn't say much
and I was like,

00:02:54.039 --> 00:02:55.641
"Yes, on the da na na na floor!

00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:56.508
The da na na na floor!

00:02:56.508 --> 00:02:58.677
Apartment da na na na na!"

00:02:58.677 --> 00:03:00.046
[ Laughter ]
And he was like,

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.381
"Uh, hello?"

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:03.249
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.484
I said, "Yes.
Da na na na floor!"

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:09.722
He said, "Uh, this Barry."

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:10.556
I said, "Barry?"

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:12.625
And I looked at all the texts.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.360
"The president's
calling you right now!

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.227
[ Laughter ]
The president!"

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:16.595
Like literally like I've missed

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.665
all my managers texting me

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:20.933
that Obama's about to call me
in a second.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:24.036
-So you thought Barack Obama
was your Postmates.

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:25.205
-Right, but wait.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:27.673
[ Laughter, cheering,
and applause ]

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:29.442
But even he said, he said,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.812
"Well, you think I'm a --
a Postmates?"

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.947
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:37.316
And I was like, "Wait, you know
what Postmates is?"

00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:40.920
[ Laughter ]
So. yeah, he was the first.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.823
-"I thought it was you
and it was Postmates.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:45.357
I just thought,
after people are presidents,

00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:46.960
they take any job they can,
you know, find."

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.760
[ Laughter ]
-You know what, though?

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.262
I was so hungry at the time,

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.531
I kind of wished
it was Postmates.

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:53.032
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:55.034
-How did you find out?
How did you find out

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:57.270
you were nominated?

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.705
-Oh, this is weird.
Okay, so,

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:02.241
you know,
around this time period,

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:08.181
like depending on how strong
your character is, as a human,

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.282
this is where you can
start getting caught

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:14.554
in kind of this endless yarn
of getting inside your head.

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:15.922
-Yes, of course.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.790
-You know, Will Smith's book
just came out

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.992
and that was really like
probably one

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.661
of the most important books
I've ever read

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.028
because, really,
no one in hip hop

00:04:23.028 --> 00:04:25.298
has allowed themselves
to be that vulnerable

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:28.834
and talk about feelings
and emotions and those things.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.937
And so I kind of stalked
Will Smith.

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.705
Even though, like we're both
from West Philly,

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.440
like it was just casual,
"Hey, how are you doing?"

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.308
But, you know, I stalked him

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:39.479
and kind of made him
my de facto life coach.

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.414
[ Laughter ]
And so, you know,

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:44.951
Will has been trying to talk me
into like waking up at 4:00.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:48.587
He wakes up every day at 4:30
in the morning and was like,

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:49.989
"Yo, you got to get up
at 4:30 in the morning.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:51.924
Watch the sunrise.
Seize the day."

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.993
You know how Will Smith gets,
like super --

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.695
[ Laughter ]
And, you know, I was just like,

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:57.396
[ Yawns ] "Oh, okay,
I'll see you at 10:00 am.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:58.798
Alright, I'll call you."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.468
And so, you know,
the day of the announcements,

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.436
I was like, "Alright, I'm going
to do this Will Smith thing

00:05:04.436 --> 00:05:05.838
and get up at 4:30
in the morning."

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:10.443
So, like imagine the whole
Tom Cruise, "Risky Business,"

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:12.478
"Old Time Rock &amp; Roll" sliding
across the dance floor,

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.180
turn off the music
and dancing in the house

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.314
in my underwear
and all that stuff,

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.715
in the shower and all
that stuff.

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:19.351
[ Laughter ]
And, you know,
I put my good suit on,

00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:20.719
got everything ready,

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:23.022
and I was sitting there waiting
for my team to call,

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:24.791
ready, like,
"Okay, let's have this."

00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:28.594
And, about 35 minutes later,

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.830
I hit my executive assistant,
Kathy, up and I was like,

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.167
"Yo, where you guys at?"

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:38.171
She's like, "Ahmir, it's Sunday.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.839
This is not until Tuesday."

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:42.307
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.307 --> 00:05:44.777
-And I just sat there
like a fool in my suit.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:48.514
Like my good suit,
my good Sunday suit, so.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:49.615
I was humiliated.

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:51.950
I went back to bed.
I was so mad, man.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:52.752
[ Laughter ]
Well, that makes sense

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.186
because I remember

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:56.121
they had a picture of you
the day you did get nominated.

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:57.824
Your suit was all wrinkly.

00:05:57.824 --> 00:05:59.425
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:02.161
The actual morning of it,
I stayed in my --

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.297
you know, all pandemic,
I lived in nothing

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:06.298
but Old Navy pajamas, so.
-There you go.

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:09.368
[ Laughter ]
One of the cool things
about this documentary,

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:11.570
you told me,
when you were last here,

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.606
is that people
were finding themselves

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:15.407
or finding their parents
-Yeah.

00:06:15.407 --> 00:06:17.810
-in the audience
of this music festival

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.645
because there had never been
footage of it before.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:20.913
Has that continued to happen

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.782
while it's gone out wider?
-Constantly.

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:27.486
Like the number one message
I get in my DMs is sort of like,

00:06:27.486 --> 00:06:29.589
"Who knew my grandmom
was a hot girl?"

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:31.056
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-You know.

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:33.626
Like that's --

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:36.496
That's always the like,
"Who knew my grandma
was a hot girl?"

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:40.699
So, yeah, there's a lot
of that happening.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:42.301
-It's so great, by the way,
for the grandma

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.270
who's always been like,
"I told you."

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.373
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:50.643
-Yeah, absolutely.
No, like notable musicians

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:52.078
that I grew up listening to,
it's like,

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:55.314
"Yo, that's my fiancé/e at the
age of 14 in the front row,"

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:58.050
that sort of thing.
And,

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.019
yeah, every day,
it's like a brand new surprise.

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:04.790
Like you never know.
Like just this week

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:08.527
at the DGAs,
the Directors Guild thing,

00:07:08.527 --> 00:07:11.663
you know, I'd kind of been
trailing Spielberg.

00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:14.099
Like now that I'm allowed
in these spaces,

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:17.036
where you got to go to these
like different awards,

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:18.938
pre-awards things,
before the Oscars.

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:21.573
You know, I've seen Spielberg
about six times,

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:22.741
but I was like really sheepish.

00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:24.810
Like in my mind, I was like,
"Okay, well,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.279
I met Rita Moreno twice,

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:28.881
so that's kind of
like meeting Spielberg."

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.951
[ Laughter ]
Kind of like meeting Gayle
eight times

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.485
is knowing Oprah.
-That's one Oprah.

00:07:33.485 --> 00:07:34.754
[ Laughter ]
-Right, exactly.

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:38.924
So, you know, after Rita
sort of recognized me

00:07:38.924 --> 00:07:40.059
the fourth time, I'm like,

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:41.927
it's kind of like
knowing Spielberg.

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:44.363
Anyway, I decided,
"Okay, this is your moment."

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:46.132
And, you know,
I went up while sheepish,

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:49.034
like the Mean Joe Green
Coke commercial from the '70s,

00:07:49.034 --> 00:07:53.105
like, "Um, sir, uh,
my name is, uh."

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:56.242
And Lin-Manuel
sort of sensing like --

00:07:56.242 --> 00:07:58.911
Lin-Manuel is now like maybe
a junior in high school,

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:01.046
so he totally looked at me
like a freshman,

00:08:01.046 --> 00:08:03.148
like he just cut to the chase.

00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:04.850
"Yo, he directed
'Summer of Soul,'"

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.219
and literally,
like Spielberg just started

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:08.887
fanning out for three minutes.

00:08:08.887 --> 00:08:11.691
[ Audience ohs ]
Like I was like, "What?!"

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:12.791
[ Laughter ]

00:08:12.791 --> 00:08:14.927
He said, "I watched it
multiple times!

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.995
And da da da da."
And I just, you know.

00:08:16.995 --> 00:08:18.564
But every day
has been like that.

00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:22.335
-That's really wonderful, man.
That's so exciting.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.036
I want to always ask,
when you're here,

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:26.939
because it's my favorite detail
about you.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:31.644
I know you have an ever-growing
collection of vinyl albums.

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:34.546
What's the number you would put
on it right now?

00:08:34.546 --> 00:08:37.116
-It's kissing 200,000.
-200,000?

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:39.518
[ Audience oohs ]
-Oh, my God.

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:41.153
Wait a minute.
-Yeah.

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:44.222
-This is like you remember
like "Good Times"?

00:08:44.222 --> 00:08:46.324
Whenever, like, you know,

00:08:46.324 --> 00:08:48.827
James Evans lost his job
and the audience starts --

00:08:48.827 --> 00:08:49.929
"Ooh!"
-"Aah!"

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:51.563
[ Laughter ]

00:08:51.563 --> 00:08:54.199
-I felt like I just delivered
bad news on "Good Times," like.

00:08:54.199 --> 00:08:56.635
[ Laughter ]
I'm fired again!
"Aah!"

00:08:56.635 --> 00:08:59.905
-They didn't react to it like,
"That's a cool thing."

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:02.040
[ Laughter ]

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.042
-They're like, "Awww!"

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:07.513
-No, but it's different now
because,

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:09.748
you know, like a lot
of libraries are closing,

00:09:09.748 --> 00:09:12.117
you know, like colleges
are losing their jazz programs.

00:09:12.117 --> 00:09:14.019
Maybe they're
all going digital.

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:17.490
So now you just got people
that are, you know,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.993
libraries that have
60,000 pieces or whatever

00:09:20.993 --> 00:09:23.061
or like, you know,
we used to have jukeboxes

00:09:23.061 --> 00:09:26.832
in diners, so all those one-stop
shop guys that had like

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.101
500 copies of "Billie Jean,"

00:09:29.101 --> 00:09:31.136
like it's just rotting
somewhere, you know.

00:09:31.136 --> 00:09:32.738
So they were like, "Oh,
that Roots guy likes records.

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:34.539
Give it to him."
[ Laughter ]

00:09:34.539 --> 00:09:37.042
My girlfriend's none too happy
about it, though, so.

00:09:37.042 --> 00:09:38.344
-But it is a great
responsibility.

00:09:38.344 --> 00:09:40.079
People give it to you
because I think

00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:42.515
there's an assumption
that you will maintain it,

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:44.717
you will keep it safe.
-They know I don't want it

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:47.052
to go in the trash, but, really,
I don't have any use

00:09:47.052 --> 00:09:49.288
for like 500 copies
of "Billie Jean."

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:51.890
But I do have 500 copies, so.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.026
-Hey, congratulations
on the film.

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:55.360
Best of luck at the Oscars.
-Thank you so much.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.229
-It's always nice to see you.
Questlove.

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:59.732
[ Cheering and applause ]
"Summer of Soul"
streaming on Hulu

00:09:59.732 --> 00:10:02.568
and the film's soundtrack
is available as well.

